            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.27,
            0.64,
            0.481,
            0.66
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ready to Go Beyond"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.665,
            0.86,
            0.807
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "During lightning strikes, a tiny amount of atmospheric nitrogen is fixed to produce nitrogen oxides. Today most of the nitrogen is fixed artificially via the Haber-Bosch process (early 1900s) of making ammonia from atmospheric nitrogen, which produces most of the fertilisers used today. This ‘Bread from Air’ revolutionised agriculture, enabling India’s Green Revolution and feeding billions. More than half the nitrogen atoms in the human body come from the Haber-Bosch process. However, this reaction is energy intensive (uses ~ 1 – 2% of global energy), and the overuse of fertilisers has degraded soil and water."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.829,
            0.445,
            0.849
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "13.3.4 Oxygen cycle"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.851,
            0.89,
            0.921
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Oxygen is one of the Earth’s most abundant elements. About 21% of the atmosphere consist of free oxygen gas (O ). It is an essential component of most biological molecules like carbohydrates, proteins, nucleic acids and fats. Oxygen also exists in combined forms — in the Earth’s crust as"
            }
          ]
        },
